module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.private_tree_inside_a_public_worktree_publishes_on_archive

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data private_tree_inside_a_public_worktree_publishes_on_archive: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "private_tree_inside_a_public_worktree_publishes_on_archive" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**A PRIVATE REPOSITORY PLACED INSIDE A PUBLIC REPOSITORY'S WORKTREE IS PUBLISHED BY ARCHIVE-TIME AUTO-PUSH, AND THE SESSION THAT PLACED IT IS NOT ALIVE TO PREVENT IT** (INVALID STATE: private content -- a clone, or an unversioned overlay COPY of a private tree -- sits under a worktree whose remote is public, and the harness's archive-time auto-push commits and pushes worktree state without asking. The author is not consulted because the author no longer exists: the session is archived first and the push happens afterwards. THE ROOT IS A RECIPE THE LANES WERE FOLLOWING, NOT AN IMPROVISATION, WHICH IS WHY IT RECURRED ACROSS UNRELATED LANES: the private-witness step of the generated CI workflow copies the private tree into the public checkout -- cp -a ../dag <name>_overlay/dag -- and that step is SAFE IN CI, where the checkout is thrown away, and UNSAFE IN A SESSION, where the worktree persists and is auto-committed. The same instruction is correct in one execution context and a disclosure in the other, and nothing in the instruction distinguishes them. MEASURED INSTANCE, 2026-09-11: session proud-hawk-390 archived at 00:35Z; at 11:15:45Z -- ELEVEN HOURS LATER -- branch session/proud-hawk-390 received a single WIP commit aba7c528 carrying 85,967 lines, base_overlay/ and private_overlay/ copies of the private repository including its finance models and its whole strategy tree. The dashboard then auto-opened a pull request against the PUBLIC repository and a reviewer reviewed the private planning document in public before any human noticed. THE DISTINGUISHING FACT, AND IT IS THE WHOLE ROW: a nested CLONE of the private repository (its own .git, at a path the public repo's .git/info/exclude names) is reported ignored and is NOT an auto-push candidate; an overlay COPY (no .git, at a path nothing excludes) IS. Five worktrees on one host carried private material that night and the two that leaked were the two that were copies at unexcluded paths -- same hazard, same session shape, opposite outcome, decided entirely by the directory name. So the protection is PATH-SHAPED while the hazard is CONTENT-SHAPED, and nothing joins them. AND THE PATH IS NOT EVEN STABLE: the overlay directory name is a PARAMETER of the recipe, so the three observed instances used three different names -- base_overlay, private_overlay, wb62_overlay -- which is why a scan written against the first two names missed the third. A survey keyed on a name is unsound against a producer that takes the name as an argument. WHY THE EXISTING PROTECTION IS A SILENT PRECONDITION RATHER THAN A GUARD: the exclusion lives in .git/info/exclude, which is NOT VERSION CONTROLLED, so it is absent in a fresh clone and in any worktree whose common git dir differs, and its absence is invisible until the push has already happened. A setup that is safe today is unsafe tomorrow for a reason no diff records. CONTAINMENT IS INCOMPLETE BY CONSTRUCTION: closing the pull request and deleting the branch leaves the commit fetchable at refs/pull/<n>/head, which GitHub retains, so the exposure survives every action available from inside the repository and its removal requires the forge operator. THREE THINGS THE FIRST CONTAINMENT MISSED AND SUCCESSIVE SWEEPS FOUND, EACH WIDENING THE POPULATION: a SECOND worktree held the same untracked overlay copy, armed and waiting for its own archive; the archived session's LOCAL branch still held the leak commit and would have re-pushed it on the next harness push; and a SECOND PUBLISHED INSTANCE existed on another branch entirely -- 117 private paths under a third overlay name, committed rather than untracked, so every clean-working-tree check and every name-keyed scan passed over it. It was found only by a widened pattern run against main's own baseline across all remote refs and open pull-request heads. A CLASS THAT RE-ARMS AFTER CONTAINMENT IS NOT CONTAINED UNTIL THE POPULATION IS SWEPT BY IDENTITY AGAINST A BASELINE, and each of the first two sweeps reported completeness it did not have. RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable -- the publication is detected after the fact and the residue is deleted where deletion reaches. CEILING: STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE, and the construction is ordinary rather than clever -- auto-push cannot publish a tree it cannot see, so private material held OUTSIDE the worktree has no constructor for this invalid state at all, and no rule, no exclusion entry and no reviewer is needed to maintain it. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, A CAPABILITY AND NOT AN ARTEFACT, AND CORRECTED ONCE ALREADY BY A SECOND INSTANCE: the private-witness step must materialise its overlay OUTSIDE the public checkout, so that neither a throwaway CI checkout nor a persistent session worktree can contain foreign private content at any moment. THE TRIGGER THIS ROW FIRST CARRIED WAS INSUFFICIENT AND IS RECORDED HERE RATHER THAN QUIETLY REPLACED: it said archive-time auto-push must refuse UNTRACKED trees. The second instance, commit 86df0fd62f on session/warm-badger-62-zero-field-hole, was ALREADY COMMITTED when it was pushed -- git status was clean -- so an untracked-tree refusal would have been satisfied while the capability stayed dead, which is the DESIGN section 4b(3) grain mismatch this ledger exists to catch, committed by this row's own author within hours of policing it elsewhere. WHAT DOES NOT RETIRE THIS ROW, stated because it is the tempting discharge and it is [[a_written_row_is_not_a_firing_mechanism]] exactly: a brief instructing lanes to clone outside the worktree, or to run a path check after cloning. Both are PUSH friction delivered to named authors, both expire when those lanes archive, and neither makes the dangerous form harder to type for the next lane that copies a tree. The verification step is worth sending and is not the capability.)",

    "**A TRACKED IGNORE DERIVED FROM REPOSITORY VISIBILITY — RUNG-2 MITIGATION, PATH-SHAPED, AND THE ROW'S TRIGGER IS UNCHANGED.** (2026-09-13. MEASURED FIRST: a directory named `private_overlay/`, `base_overlay/`, `wb62_overlay/` or `privdag/` placed in a PUBLIC gunbc session worktree stages FULL FILE CONTENT on `git add -A` with no warning, mode 100644; a nested clone at `private_repo/` stages a gitlink, 160000, with a warning. The tracked `.gitignore` carried no entry for any of them, so whatever protected a given session was a `.git/info/exclude` local exclude — the silent precondition this row already names. A name-only sweep of the public repository found no private-shaped path, so nothing had leaked, and the vector was open by default in every session. WHAT LANDED, AND THE SHAPE MATTERS MORE THAN THE ROWS: the privacy fact is NOT authored beside the ignore. `extdeps.github.repository` now carries ONE `RepositoryVisibility` — it was forked into `actions_token` `RepositoryVisibility` and `hosted_runners` `RepoVisibility`, one concept under two spellings — and `gunbc.repository` carries `gunb-ai/gunbc` and `gunb-ai/gunbc-private` as typed `Repository` rows -- ONE authority for the instance, consumed rather than restated, and the private row's fields were read from the GitHub repository object rather than assumed -- with `extdeps.github.repository` `repository_visibility` mapping the API's boolean alias to the typed coproduct, so `not world-readable` is asked of one row in one place. `gunbc.workspace_repository_materialisation` declares the COMPOSE CONTRACT — which repository is materialised under which prefix, by which method — and derives the staged shape from the method rather than authoring it, because a row carrying both could declare a clone that stages full content. `gunbc.gitignore_emit` then DERIVES one ignore row per materialisation whose source repository is not world-readable, with the comment text derived from that visibility. SO A NEW OVERLAY SPELLING IS A COMPOSE ROW AND THE IGNORE FOLLOWS BY CONSTRUCTION, and flipping a repository to public at the forge removes its rows from the projection without anyone editing an ignore list. THE PUBLIC HALF ONLY: the private repository's `strategy.private_witness_workflow` `overlay_local_method` is the other half of the contract and is cited by name, never imported. WHAT RUNG THIS IS AND WHY IT IS NOT HIGHER: MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE (rung 2) AT BEST, and the derivation does not change that — it removes a second representation of the privacy fact, which is a §3 defect, not a §4b rung. `git add -f` bypasses a tracked ignore entirely, and archive-time auto-push has already been watched committing ALREADY-COMMITTED private content, which no ignore reaches. THE GUARD IS PATH-SHAPED WHILE THE HAZARD IS CONTENT-SHAPED — the same mismatch this row's first receipt names — and the materialisation prefix is a PARAMETER of the producing recipe, so the rostered prefixes are the ones that have been OBSERVED and not the set that can occur: the next argument nobody has typed yet is covered by nothing here. THE DERIVATION MAKES ADDING A SPELLING ONE ROW; IT DOES NOT MAKE AN UNTYPED SPELLING SAFE. AND IT MUST STAY ENROLLED: deleting a materialisation row, or flipping a visibility standing that is not true at the forge, is a silent reopening, which is the enrollment dependence that keeps rung 2 below the ceiling. THE TRIGGER IS UNTOUCHED AND IS RESTATED HERE ONLY TO SAY SO: the next-rung trigger remains that the private-witness step must materialise its overlay OUTSIDE the public checkout. This receipt discharges NOTHING of it — an ignore that names prefixes inside the worktree is by construction not the capability that removes the material from the worktree, and a receipt that quietly narrowed the trigger to what it had just built would be the DESIGN §4b(3) grain mismatch this row was corrected for once already.)",

    "**THE PROTECTION THAT WAS ACTUALLY WORKING WAS THE ONE NOBODY COULD SEE, AND IT WAS SIMULTANEOUSLY TOO NARROW TO TRAVEL AND TOO BROAD WHERE IT APPLIED** (measured 2026-09-14 by quick-owl-739 and re-verified independently on the same host). WHAT COVERED THE OVERLAY PREFIX BEFORE THIS CHANGE: a single machine-local file, the COMMON git directory's info/exclude, carrying private_overlay/ and private_overlay_m/. Every linked worktree on the host reads it, so `git check-ignore -v` in a composed worktree returned a reassuring answer naming that file -- and the answer was reassuring FOR THE WRONG REASON. The file is uncommitted, unreviewed, invisible to every diff and every review, and ABSENT IN A FRESH CLONE, so a lane that verified its own safety by running check-ignore was reading a fact about one machine and concluding a fact about the repository. That is the silent-precondition arm this row's first receipt names, caught in the act rather than inferred. THE SAME FILE IS ALSO OVER-BROAD, WHICH IS THE HALF NOBODY WAS LOOKING FOR: its private_overlay/ has no leading slash, so `git check-ignore -v fixtures/private_overlay/example.dag` resolves to it -- the local protection swallows NESTED material that no compose step ever created and that may be perfectly public. A guard can fail in both directions at once, and this one did. WHAT THE COMMITTED ROWS DO ABOUT EACH HALF: they travel, because they are tracked and derived from gunbc.workspace_repository_materialisation; and they are ANCHORED at the workspace root and WITHOUT a trailing slash -- /private_overlay, not private_overlay/ (git reads a trailing-slash pattern as directory-only, so it would not cover a symlink at that path) -- because a materialisation row declares a prefix AT THE ROOT while git reads an unanchored pattern as matching at any depth. The anchor carries its own discriminating witness pair in test.claim.gitignore_gate, a root path ignored and a nested same-named path NOT ignored, because an unanchored pattern passes every root-level check while quietly widening the scope. The machine-local exclude keeps its over-broad form and is outside this repository's authority to fix. private_overlay_m/ gets NO row: quick-owl-739 confirms it was never observed being produced by anything and appears only in that exclude, and an exclude line proves someone once wanted a path ignored and nothing more -- a row asserts a compose step materialises the private repository there, which no observation supports. THREE STAGING BEHAVIOURS ARE NOW MEASURED, NOT TWO, and the third was silent: a nested CLONE stages a gitlink and `git add -A` WARNS; a COPY stages full file content at mode 100644, silently; a SYMLINK stages mode 120000 whose blob body is the TARGET PATH, with NO warning at all. The symlink leaks a filesystem path rather than private bytes, so it is materially less severe and is not dressed up as a leak here; it was produced by an earlier recipe today and is discontinued, which is why it carries no materialisation row. AND THE FIRST ATTEMPT TO DISMISS THE SYMLINK WAS ITSELF A DEFECT, RECORDED HERE BECAUSE THE REASONING IS THE SEDUCTIVE KIND AND WILL BE OFFERED AGAIN. The receipt originally argued that no method arm was needed because the ignore rows are derived from VISIBILITY and PREFIX alone, so one prefix row covers every staging behaviour at that prefix. The premise is true and the conclusion did not follow: the DERIVATION is method-agnostic, but COVERAGE is decided by the emitted PATTERN, and a gitignore pattern ending in a slash matches DIRECTORIES ONLY. Measured in a scratch repository: with `/private_repo/` committed, a symlink named private_repo stages as mode 120000, straight past the pattern. A true statement about one layer was carried into a claim about another, and the gap was invisible because both sentences are about the same row. WHAT CLOSED IT is the pattern form rather than a new arm: the materialisation row now carries a bare PATH PREFIX and the emitter owns the punctuation, so the emitted pattern has no trailing slash and matches a file, a directory and a symlink at that path -- each of the three measured, with a nested same-named path still spared. A method arm is owed when a LIVE recipe adopts a method, not before. AND THE IGNORE ROW IS ONE WALL OF SEVERAL, which is the last measured fact and belongs to the compose instruction rather than here: `git worktree prune` DOES NOT DELETE FILES, and four pruned compose worktrees were found still holding full private tree copies on disk -- unstageable by that repository, but present, and invisible to a sweep that enumerates `git worktree list`. Teardown must therefore be `git worktree remove`, or a removal of the detached directory, never prune; and a sweep for residue must be a FILESYSTEM sweep. Nothing in this repository's ignore rows reaches a file sitting outside every worktree it knows about.)",

    "**THE ADJACENT ROWS AND WHY NEITHER OWNS THIS CLASS.** [[a_branch_property_falsified_by_a_derived_push]] is about a push INVALIDATING A PROPERTY ASSERTED OF A BRANCH; here no property is falsified -- the push is faithful to the worktree and that is precisely the defect, because the worktree contained something that was never meant to be a publication subject. [[publish_over_a_path_a_reader_is_serving]] is about publication COLLIDING WITH A CONCURRENT READER over one path; here there is no collision and no reader, and the harm is that the publication happened at all, to an audience defined by the remote's visibility rather than by any path. A THIRD BOUNDARY IS OFTEN ASSERTED HERE AND IT IS NOT ON RECORD: auto-push publishing PARTIAL worktree state -- a half-finished change escaping as if finished -- has NO ROW IN THIS DIRECTORY, and this receipt originally claimed it did. The claim came from an operator-session memory rather than from the corpus, which is a citation to something no reader can reach, in the one receipt whose whole job is to establish that no existing class owns this one. It is recorded rather than deleted because the near-miss is the useful part: an unresolvable boundary reads exactly like a checked one, and the row demonstrated the resolvable form two sentences earlier. The partial-state class remains UNFILED; whoever files it owns that boundary, and until then no reader may treat this row as distinguished from it. THIS row is about state that is COMPLETE, CORRECT, AND FOREIGN: the bytes are exactly right and belong to a different repository with a different visibility, so no completeness check and no staging discipline reaches it. The composition -- a private repository nested inside a public one -- is what is new, and it is what makes an ordinary archive into a disclosure.",
    "**FOURTH INSTANCE, AND IT ADDS TWO MECHANICAL FACTS THE ROW DID NOT CARRY (snappy-pike-154, 2026-09-13, CONTAINED -- no commit created, nothing pushed).** A lane composing the private witness roster ran `cp -r <private>/dag privdag` INSIDE a session worktree whose remote is public gunb-ai/gunbc. It stood about ten minutes and was deleted; containment verified independently by the lane and by its manager -- branch head unchanged at a95cf2f4ee0, `git status` clean, and a name-only read of the last eight commits grepped for privdag, private_overlay, base_overlay, dag/strategy/ and finance paths returning zero. FIRST NEW FACT, THE STEER: the operator did not improvise the unsafe placement, THE COMPILER SENT THEM THERE. `gunbc run` panics `anchor_source_root: source root private_overlay/dag resolved to <path> which is not a directory (process workspace <worktree>)`, and `strategy.private_witness_workflow` `private_entry_prefix` is the string \"private_overlay/\" -- so the error message names the required layout, INSIDE the workspace, and says nothing about where that layout is safe. The safe local recipe already exists and is four lines below the unsafe line in the same generated workflow: compose in a DETACHED worktree outside the session worktree (`git worktree add --detach <scratch> HEAD`, overlay inside that), or in a remote-less scratch repo. So the row's standing sentence -- the instruction does not distinguish its contexts -- is understated: the distinguishing sentence EXISTS and is simply not co-located with the instruction it qualifies, while the failure path is the one the diagnostic advertises. SECOND NEW FACT, AND IT RETIRES `.git/info/exclude` AS A MITIGATION IN WORKTREES ENTIRELY: the lane DID write the exclusion and it did nothing. Measured with a probe directory in the same worktree -- appending the path to `$(git rev-parse --git-dir)/info/exclude`, the PER-WORKTREE gitdir, leaves it `?? untracked`; appending the same line to `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/info/exclude` excludes it. In a worktree `--git-dir` and `--git-common-dir` differ, `info/exclude` is honoured only from the common dir, and the obvious command hands you the file git never reads. This is not the row's existing \"path-shaped protection\" observation weakened by a missing entry; it is an entry written correctly by the operator's lights into a file with no effect, with no warning at write time and no signal until the push. And the overlay name was a FOURTH spelling -- `privdag` -- after base_overlay, private_overlay and wb62_overlay, confirming the row's point that a name-keyed scan is unsound against a producer that takes the name as an argument. A MITIGATION ON THE WAY TO THIS ROW'S EXISTING TRIGGER, AND EXPLICITLY NOT A REPLACEMENT FOR IT (review 65500, DESIGN 4b(3) grain). The repair these two facts suggest is that the placement diagnostic which names `private_overlay/dag` should also name the safe composition, so the message that CAUSES the unsafe copy is the message that prevents it. That is worth building and it is RUNG 1: an operator-facing message can be ignored, misread, or arrive after the copy, and it does not remove the constructor for the invalid state. THIS ROW'S TRIGGER IS UNCHANGED and remains the capability it already carries -- the private-witness step materialises its overlay OUTSIDE the public checkout, so that neither a throwaway CI checkout nor a persistent session worktree can contain foreign private content at any moment, and auto-push cannot publish a tree it cannot see. This receipt does not retire the row and must not be read as narrowing what does. The row already rules that instructions and path checks do not discharge it; a better-worded diagnostic is the same kind of thing, and the author of this receipt proposed it as the trigger while quoting the sentence that forbids exactly that -- which is itself the 4b(3) grain mismatch the ledger exists to catch, committed inside a receipt about being steered into a hazard",

  ],

  evidence: [],
}
